+# (sky130A/sky130_procs.tcl, issue 0429).
+#
+# ============================================================================
+# ⚠ I6 IS THE WHOLE RISK OF THIS FILE, AND IT IS FIVE THINGS
+# ============================================================================
+# The walk sets no_draw 1, no_undo 1, keep_symbols 1, pushes a log-suppress
+# scope and DESCENDS THE REAL DESIGN. Every exit path — including a raise three
+# levels down — restores all of them AND the entry hierarchy level. Before-state
+# on this tree: `sky130_save_fet_params` raises `Symbol not found` and returns
+# with no_draw=1 keep_symbols=1 still set (issue 0431). Shape:
+# `catch {…} res opts` -> an unconditional `_restore` whose every line is
+# individually catch-wrapped -> `return -options $opts $res`.
+#
+#  * THE UNWIND IS BOUNDED BY THE ENTRY LEVEL, not by 0. src/xschem.tcl:3857's
+#    `while {[xschem get currsch]} {xschem go_back}` would ascend past a caller
+#    that was already descended, leaving the user in a different cell than the
+#    one they invoked this from.
+#  * `xschem get no_undo` DOES NOT EXIST (setter only, scheduler.c:11958; it
+#    returns {} whether the flag is 0 or 1 — measured). So the entry value is
+#    unobservable and 0 is the only value that can be restored. Issue 0432.
+#  * AN UNPOPPED SUPPRESS SCOPE SILENCES THE USER'S ACTION LOG for the rest of
+#    the session — a worse defect than the navigation noise it removes — so the
+#    pop lives in the same unconditional block as the flags. The scope also
+#    covers the ORACLE: `xschem netlist` self-logs (scheduler.c:8887), and a
+#    read-only annotation item has no business writing a `netlist` line into a
+#    log whose contract is REPLAYABLE USER EDITS. (`-keep_symbols` silences it
+#    too; both, because either alone is one edit away from being removed.)
+#  * THE ORACLE RUNS BEFORE `no_undo 1`, deliberately. global_spice_netlist()
+#    push_undo()s the document and pop_undo()s it back (spice_netlist.c, see the
+#    0263 comment in scheduler.c); running that round trip with undo disarmed is
+#    not a state this code has any reason to explore.
+#
+# ============================================================================
+# ⚠ `xschem descend` RETURNS 0 IN TWO CLASSES WITH OPPOSITE RESPONSES (0250)
+# ============================================================================
+# Measured on this tree (actions.c:4055-4065): a refusal BEFORE descending
+# (`no-selection`, `maxdepth`, `not-descendable:<type>`) returns 0 with currsch
+# UNCHANGED, while a blank/missing schematic returns 0 with currsch ALREADY
+# INCREMENTED (`load-failed`). Both PDK prototypes answer 0 with an
+# unconditional `go_back 2` (sg13g2_procs.tcl:401-407): right for the second
+# class, and for the first it pops a level it never pushed — at the top level
+# go_back is a no-op so nobody noticed, but one level down it corrupts sch_path
+# for the rest of the walk and the walk re-visits levels, emitting DUPLICATE
+# cards. Reachable for real at CADMAXHIER=40 on a recursive design. Issue 0433.
+# So the decision is driven off `xschem get currsch` before/after, NEVER off
+# descend's return value. Both prototypes still carry the bug; S5 deletes them.
+#
+# RESIDUAL, unmeasured: a VECTOR DEVICE instance (`name=M1[3:0]` on a symbol
+# that has a descriptor) gets ONE card set built from the literal bracketed
+# name. Both prototypes have the same hole. Issue 0439.
+
+namespace eval op_annot {
+  ## Cards accumulated by the CURRENT walk, and the warnings from the most
+  ## recent one. Namespace state rather than a `global <pdk>_sch_expand` array
+  ## (the prototype's shape) so nothing leaks into the user's global namespace.
+  variable _acc
+  variable warnings
+  ## The re-entrancy latch (issue 0438). Attempt 2 left 0438 open when the only
+  ## shared state was the accumulator; the oracle adds a netlist inside a
+  ## netlist and a saved/forced netlist environment, and a nested call would
+  ## "restore" the outer walk's settings to the already-forced ones.
+  variable _busy
+  ## The two aggregate counters (decision N8). Counts, never per-device lines.
+  variable _miss_dev
+  variable _miss_sub
+  if {![info exists _acc]}      { set _acc {} }
+  if {![info exists warnings]}  { set warnings {} }
+  if {![info exists _busy]}     { set _busy 0 }
+  if {![info exists _miss_dev]} { set _miss_dev 0 }
+  if {![info exists _miss_sub]} { set _miss_sub 0 }
+}
+
+## op_annot::last_warnings -> the list of human-readable warnings from the most
+## recent save_cards, {} when it was clean.
+##
+## The prototype's answer to an undescendable instance is a bare
+## `puts "Can not descend into $instname"` (sg13g2_procs.tcl:405), which is
+## invisible under --nogui, unassertable from a test, and lost in a GUI session
+## where nobody watches stdout. A collected list can be shown, tested, or
+## ignored by the caller.
+##
+## ⚠ TWO AGGREGATES, NEVER A PER-DEVICE FLOOD (decision D7 kept, N8 added).
+## `spice_ignore` is the ordinary way to keep a documentation-only device on a
+## sheet and one line each would bury the one warning that matters — but a
+## filter that UNDER-emits in silence is the failure that survived 85 and then
+## 96 green checks, so the counts are reported: how many devices a descriptor
+## claimed that the netlist does not contain, and how many subcircuit instances
+## the netlist does not expand. Cells the walk could not ENTER (a descend
+## refusal) still get their own line, because that is a walk failure and not a
+## filter decision.
+proc op_annot::last_warnings {} {
+  variable warnings
+  return $warnings
+}
+
+## ============================================================================
+## THE ORACLE SEAMS. NINE PROCS, BECAUSE EACH ONE CAN BE WRONG ON ITS OWN.
+## ============================================================================
+
+## Where the throwaway deck is written. A NAME, so a test can prove no temp file
+## survives and can force the "no deck was written" path without a broken PDK.
+##
+## op_annot's OWN directory, never `$netlist_dir`: the user's netlist directory
+## holds the deck they are about to simulate and a menu item that reads a
+## schematic may not put a file next to it — nor may it inherit
+## `local_netlist_dir`, which sends every write into `<design dir>/simulation/`
+## (src/xschem.tcl:9008 via simuldir, measured).
+proc op_annot::_oracle_dir {} {
+  if {[info exists ::USER_CONF_DIR] && [string trim $::USER_CONF_DIR] ne {}} {
+    return [file join $::USER_CONF_DIR op_annot]
+  }
+  if {[info exists ::env(HOME)] && [string trim $::env(HOME)] ne {}} {
+    return [file join $::env(HOME) .xschem op_annot]
+  }
+  return [file join [pwd] .op_annot]
+}
+
+## Snapshot of every netlist global the oracle forces OR PERTURBS, INCLUDING
+## whether it existed at all. Six entries of {name existed value}: four Tcl
+## globals, then two pieces of C-side state read and written through
+## `xschem get/set`.
+##
+## ⚠ `netlist_name` IS IN THIS LIST ALTHOUGH THE ORACLE NEVER FORCES IT, AND
+## THAT IS THE POINT (issue 0443, decision D9). `xschem netlist <file>` SETS
+## xctx->netlist_name from the filename it was handed (scheduler.c:8768) and
+## then, with erc==0 — which the oracle's netlist always is — CLEARS it
+## (scheduler.c:8841). MEASURED: a user who typed a custom netlist name lost it
+## to a read-only annotation click, and on the RAISING path it was worse still,
+## left reading op_annot's own `op_annot_oracle_<pid>.spice`. A snapshot list
+## that covers only what we deliberately force is the wrong list — what matters
+## is what the run CHANGES. I4's read-only discipline covers editor state and
+## not only the .sch.
+proc op_annot::_netlist_env {} {
+  set snap {}
+  foreach v {netlist_dir local_netlist_dir flat_netlist split_files} {
+    if {[info exists ::$v]} {
+      lappend snap [list $v 1 [set ::$v]]
+    } else {
+      lappend snap [list $v 0 {}]
+    }
+  }
+  foreach v {netlist_type netlist_name} {
+    if {[catch {xschem get $v} cv]} {
+      lappend snap [list $v 0 {}]
+    } else {
+      lappend snap [list $v 1 $cv]
+    }
+  }
+  return $snap
+}
+
+## Force the deck SHAPE the parser can read, and only that.
+##
+##   netlist_type spice   skip_instance() branches on xctx->netlist_type
+##                        (netlist.c:1247) and a verilog/spectre deck has no
+##                        `.subckt` blocks and no `** sch_path:` markers at all.
+##   flat_netlist 0       flatten.awk (src/xschem.tcl:2274) rewrites the deck
+##                        with NO `.subckt` and every device renamed and
+##                        UPPERCASED, so a block index of it is meaningless.
+##   split_files 0        sub-blocks go to separate per-cell files.
+##   local_netlist_dir 0  otherwise set_netlist_dir(1,dir) throws the requested
+##                        directory away and writes into the DESIGN tree.
+##
+## ⚠ `lvs_ignore` IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. It is different in kind: it changes
+## which devices the user's OWN run will contain, so forcing it would emit cards
+## for devices their deck drops. Read it, never write it — and the oracle reads
+## it for free by simply running the netlist under the user's own setting.
+## `top_is_subckt`, `lvs_netlist` and `uppercase_subckt` are inherited too: the
+## parser handles both top-block delimiters and both cases.
+proc op_annot::_force_netlist_env {dir} {
+  set ::netlist_dir $dir
+  set ::local_netlist_dir 0
+  set ::flat_netlist 0
+  set ::split_files 0
+  catch {xschem set netlist_type spice}
+  return {}
+}
+
+## THE ONE RESTORER of the forced netlist environment, on every path out of the
+## oracle — including the one where the netlist could not be written at all.
+proc op_annot::_restore_netlist_env {snap} {
+  foreach row $snap {
+    set v   [lindex $row 0]
+    set had [lindex $row 1]
+    set val [lindex $row 2]
+    ## The C-side pair. `xschem set netlist_name {}` is a real restore, not a
+    ## no-op: an EMPTY name is the tree's own "use the default" state and a
+    ## restore that skipped it would fabricate a name the user never typed
+    ## (the second leg of row T31).
+    if {$v eq {netlist_type} || $v eq {netlist_name}} {
+      if {$had} { catch {xschem set $v $val} }
+      continue
+    }
+    if {$had} {
+      catch {set ::$v $val}
+    } else {
+      catch {unset ::$v}
+    }
+  }
+  return {}
+}
+
+## Can this directory hold the throwaway deck at all?  A NAMED SEAM, and it is
+## checked BEFORE `xschem netlist` is ever issued (decision D11).
+##
+## ⚠ THE REASON IS A MODAL DIALOG NO FLAG REACHES. `xschem netlist <path>` with
+## a slash in it goes through set_netlist_dir(1, <dirname>), and a failing
+## `file mkdir` THERE pops a raw `tk_messageBox` (src/xschem.tcl:9037, and :8998
+## for the what==0 arm) that `-noalert` does not touch and that names a
+## directory the USER NEVER CHOSE. A user with a read-only HOME, or with a file
+## sitting where `~/.xschem/op_annot` should be, would get that dialog INSTEAD
+## of this module's careful named diagnostic — and an unattended X run would
+## block on it forever. MEASURED: four such dialogs inside one save_cards on the
+## suite's unwritable-directory row, and a 240-second hang with no result.
+##
+## `file writable` on the DIRECTORY, not a probe write: a probe would leave a
+## file behind on the path where the whole point is that nothing is left behind.
+proc op_annot::_oracle_dir_ready {dir} {
+  catch {file mkdir $dir}
+  if {![file isdirectory $dir]} { return 0 }
+  if {![file writable $dir]}    { return 0 }
+  return 1
+}
+
+## THE ONE PLACE the oracle's netlist is issued. A proc of its own so the "the
+## netlist ran and wrote nothing" branch below stays reachable from a test after
+## _oracle_dir_ready short-circuits the unwritable-directory case (decision D12)
+## — an unreachable branch is a green check certifying nothing, which is the
+## exact shape that has now reverted this step twice.
+##
+## `-keep_symbols` for the same reason xschem.tcl's own machinery passes it:
+## this netlist is a READ, not the user's netlist verb. It also keeps the branch
+## from self-logging (scheduler.c:8887), a seam this module DEPENDS ON and does
+## NOT OWN — its guardians are tests/headless/test_netlist_log.tcl:152-157 and
+## the source-grep row in test_selflog_grep_guard.tcl.
+##
+## `-noalert` because this netlist is INVISIBLE to the user: they clicked an
+## annotation item, not Netlist. MEASURED under X — a subcircuit whose .sch
+## cannot be opened makes load_schematic() pop a MODAL dialog (save.c:4416), and
+## the netlister threads its `alert` flag all the way down to it
+## (spice_netlist.c:695), so `-noalert` is the netlister's own answer for a
+## background read. ⚠ IT IS NOT COMPLETE: the Tcl-side `has_included_subcircuit`
+## (src/xschem.tcl:2239) pops an `alert_` that NO flag reaches, so a
+## `spice_sym_def` whose text has no matching `.subckt` still blocks an
+## unattended X run — issue 0446, filed not worked around.
+##
+## ⚠ NEVER RAISES, AND ITS RETURN VALUE IS NOT A FAILURE SIGNAL. Measured, the
+## verb answers 1 for an unconnected net and for a `spice_sym_def` with no
+## matching `.subckt`, both of which still write a complete deck. The ONLY
+## failure test is whether a deck file exists, which is the caller's.
+proc op_annot::_oracle_run {out} {
+  if {[catch {xschem netlist -keep_symbols -noalert $out} nres]} {
+    set nres "raised: $nres"
+  }
+  return $nres
+}
+
+## Run the netlist the design would actually simulate FROM WHERE WE STAND, and
+## return the deck as text. RAISES when no deck was written.
+##
+## ⚠ IT NEVER RETURNS {} ON FAILURE. An empty answer would build an empty index,
+## find no instance in it, emit zero cards and report success — indistinguishable
+## from "no PDK descriptor claims anything here" (decision N6).
+##
+## ⚠ THE RETURN VALUE OF `xschem netlist` IS NOT CONSULTED as a failure signal:
+## measured, it is 1 for an unconnected net and for a `spice_sym_def` with no
+## matching `.subckt`, both of which still write a complete deck.
+proc op_annot::_oracle_deck {} {
+  set dir [::op_annot::_oracle_dir]
+  set out [file join $dir "op_annot_oracle_[pid].spice"]
+  set snap [::op_annot::_netlist_env]
+  set body {}
+  set rc [catch {
+    ## ⚠ PRE-CHECKED, NOT ATTEMPTED. An unwritable/unmakeable oracle directory
+    ## must reach the user as THIS proc's named diagnostic, not as
+    ## `can't create directory …: not a directory` from a Tcl primitive three
+    ## frames down and NOT as set_netlist_dir's modal tk_messageBox naming a
+    ## directory the user never chose (see _oracle_dir_ready). The caller's whole
+    ## job is to tell a broken oracle apart from "nothing here is annotated", and
+    ## only the named message does that.
+    if {![::op_annot::_oracle_dir_ready $dir]} {
+      error "op_annot: the netlist ORACLE has nowhere to write. \"$dir\" is not\
+ a writable directory. The save block is not generated rather than generated\
+ empty: a card for a device the deck does not contain makes ngspice write no\
+ raw file at all."
+    }
+    catch {file delete -force $out}
+    ::op_annot::_force_netlist_env $dir
+    ## THE NETLIST, through its own named seam (see _oracle_run above).
+    set nres [::op_annot::_oracle_run $out]
+    if {![file isfile $out]} {
+      error "op_annot: the netlist ORACLE wrote no deck to \"$out\"\
+ (xschem netlist answered \"$nres\"). The save block is not generated rather\
+ than generated empty: a card for a device the deck does not contain makes\
+ ngspice write no raw file at all."
+    }
+    set fh [open $out r]
+    set body [read $fh]
+    close $fh
+  } res opts]
+  catch {file delete -force $out}
+  ::op_annot::_restore_netlist_env $snap
+  if {$rc} { return -options $opts $res }
+  return $body
+}
+
+## THE ONE PATH NORMALIZER, used on BOTH sides of every comparison.
+##
+## `regsub {\(.*}` then `abs_sym_path` is the Tcl twin of the C's
+## sanitized_abs_sym_path (actions.c:462) — a generator cell is spelled
+## `foo(a,b)` and its path is `foo`. `file normalize` on top of it is not
+## belt-and-braces: measured, `xschem get_sch_from_sym` answers
+## `…/lib/../lib/psub.sch` for an instance placed as `../lib/psub.sym` while the
+## deck's `** sch_path:` says `…/lib/psub.sch`, and abs_sym_path alone does NOT
+## collapse the `..`. Without this the cell's whole subtree is silently dropped.
+proc op_annot::_normkey {p} {
+  if {[string trim $p] eq {}} { return {} }
+  regsub {\(.*} [string trim $p] {} q
+  if {[catch {abs_sym_path $q {}} r]} { set r $q }
+  if {[string trim $r] eq {}} { set r $q }
+  if {[catch {file normalize $r} n]} { return $r }
+  return $n
+}
+
+## The parser's ONE hand-written rule, kept in its own proc so it can be named
+## in a sabotage variant: is the parser inside the deck's user-architecture
+## region after this line?
+##
+## ⚠ ITS FALSE-POSITIVE DIRECTION IS THE DANGEROUS ONE, AND IT IS MEASURED. A
+## `code_shown` / `netlist_commands` symbol puts the user's own literal text
+## INSIDE the enclosing block, between `**** begin user architecture code` and
+## `**** end user architecture code`. A symbol whose value is the line
+## `xghost net1 pmodel` would otherwise be read as proof that an instance named
+## GHOST — carrying `spice_ignore=true` and nowhere in the deck — is present,
+## and the walk would emit cards for it: issue 0442's defect arriving from the
+## parser instead of from a mirror.
+##
+## ⚠ THE PATTERNS ARE ANCHORED AND THE `*`s ARE ESCAPED. Written as
+## `string match {****begin*}` every `*` is a WILDCARD, so the pattern means
+## "any line containing begin" and the `end` twin then swallows `.ends`, leaving
+## the previous block open.
+proc op_annot::_deck_user_region {line inside} {
+  if {[string match {\*\*\*\* begin user architecture*} $line]} { return 1 }
+  if {[string match {\*\*\*\* end user architecture*} $line]}   { return 0 }
+  return $inside
+}
+
+## The deck, as a dict: normalized absolute .sch path -> list of lowercased
+## element names, in deck order. A block that exists and is EMPTY is a key with
+## an empty list — that is how `spice_stop` shows up, and it is the only thing
+## that tells "the call is in the deck but the body is not" apart from "the
+## whole instance is in the deck".
+##
+## ⚠ KEYED ON `** sch_path:`, NOT ON THE CELL NAME and NOT ON `** sym_path:`.
+##   * the cell NAME is precisely the get_cell() hash the netlister itself
+##     dedups blocks on (spice_netlist.c:487), so two same-basename cells from
+##     different libraries collapse onto one block — keying on it would make the
+##     second cell's devices look present (issue 0445 is the measured case, and
+##     under-emitting there is the I3-safe direction);
+##   * `** sym_path:` is measurably wrong for a `schematic=` polymorphic
+##     instance: get_additional_symbols (actions.c:3356) synthesizes the symbol,
+##     and the block carries the OVERRIDDEN .sch with the ORIGINAL .sym.
+##
+## Line classes, all measured on real decks: `* expanding   symbol:` resets the
+## pending key (a block with no `** sch_path:` at all — `spice_sym_def` — must
+## not inherit the previous one), `**`-prefixed top-block delimiters are the
+## same `.subckt`/`.ends` after the marker is stripped, and `*` comments, `.`
+## dot-cards and `+` continuations (break.awk splits element lines over 130
+## chars) are not elements.
+proc op_annot::_deck_index {text} {
+  set blocks {}
+  set cur     {}
+  set pending {}
+  set inuser  0
+  foreach line [split $text "\n"] {
+    set t [string trim $line]
+    if {$t eq {}} { continue }
+    set inuser [::op_annot::_deck_user_region $t $inuser]
+    ## ⚠ THE REGION TEST RUNS BEFORE THE `.subckt`/`.ends` TESTS, NOT AFTER
+    ## THEM (issue 0447). A `code_shown` / `netlist_commands` symbol may put a
+    ## COMPLETE `.subckt`/`.ends` pair inside the user-architecture region —
+    ## a helper subcircuit is ordinary practice — and with this line below the
+    ## delimiter tests that inner `.ends` ran `set cur {}` and EVERY ELEMENT
+    ## AFTER IT IN THE ENCLOSING BLOCK was silently dropped. MEASURED both
+    ## ways: the old order answers `xreal` and loses `xafter`; the two parsers
+    ## are byte-identical over all 38 shipped xschem_library/examples designs,
+    ## including the 97-block 0_examples_top.
+    ##
+    ## REJECTED: a `.subckt` nesting-depth counter. A `.subckt` inside a user
+    ## region is not nesting, it is opaque user text the netlister never
+    ## expands, so a depth model would be modelling a structure that does not
+    ## exist (decision D10).
+    if {$inuser} { continue }
+    if {[regexp {^\*\* sch_path:[ \t]*(.*)$} $t -> p]} {
+      set pending [::op_annot::_normkey $p]
+      continue
+    }
+    if {[string match {\* expanding*} $t]} { set pending {} ; set cur {} ; continue }
+    ## the deck top is `**.subckt` / `**.ends`; everything below it is bare.
+    set d $t
+    if {[string range $d 0 1] eq {**}} { set d [string trim [string range $d 2 end]] }
+    if {[regexp -nocase {^\.subckt[ \t]} $d]} {
+      set cur $pending
+      if {$cur ne {} && ![dict exists $blocks $cur]} { dict set blocks $cur {} }
+      continue
+    }
+    if {[regexp -nocase {^\.ends} $d]} { set cur {} ; set pending {} ; continue }
+    if {$cur eq {}} { continue }
+    set c [string index $t 0]
+    if {$c eq {*} || $c eq {.} || $c eq {+}} { continue }
+    ## ⚠ regexp, NOT `lindex`: a deck line is not a Tcl list and an unbalanced
+    ## brace or quote in a parameter would make lindex raise mid-parse.
+    if {![regexp {^([^ \t]+)} $t -> tok]} { continue }
+    dict lappend blocks $cur [string tolower $tok]
+  }
+  return $blocks
+}
+
+## The instance's identity IN THE DECK: `@spiceprefix@name`, lowercased.
+##
+## ⚠ `xschem translate`, NEVER `getprop instance <n> spiceprefix`. getprop reads
+## inst->prop_ptr only, so it is EMPTY whenever the token lives in the symbol
+## `template=` — issue 0430, and the reason both PDK prototypes lose the leading
+## `x` on 3 of 45 shipped sky130 cells. Measured on the section-T fixture:
+## getprop answers {} while the deck really does say `XMTOP`.
+##
+## These are the same two tokens every descriptor's devpath already
+## concatenates, which is what makes the membership test and the CARD agree by
+## construction: if they disagree the card was already wrong, and the wrong card
+## then becomes an absent card (blank, I3) instead of a raw-destroying one.
+proc op_annot::_element {instname} {
+  if {[catch {xschem translate $instname {@spiceprefix@name}} e]} { return {} }
+  return [string tolower [string trim $e]]
+}
+
+## Every element name ONE instance can appear under in the deck.
+##
+## ⚠ A VECTOR INSTANCE IS THE ORACLE'S FIRST TRAP. The netlister writes one
+## element line PER MEMBER (`x2[1]`, `x2[0]`) while the instance's own name is
+## the bracketed RANGE `x2[1:0]`, which appears in the deck nowhere. A bare
+## comparison answers 0 for every vector instance and the walk then refuses to
+## descend into it — a plausible-looking short block with nothing to say so.
+## `xschem expandlabel` is the same tool the member loop below already uses.
+proc op_annot::_elements {instname} {
+  set e [::op_annot::_element $instname]
+  if {$e eq {}} { return {} }
+  if {[string first {[} $e] < 0} { return [list $e] }
+  if {[catch {xschem expandlabel $e} x]} { return [list $e] }
+  set names [lindex [split $x { }] 0]
+  set out {}
+  foreach n [split $names {,}] {
+    if {[string trim $n] ne {}} { lappend out [string tolower [string trim $n]] }
+  }
+  if {[llength $out] == 0} { return [list $e] }
+  return $out
+}
+
+## The block of the cell we are STANDING IN, or {} when the deck has none.
+proc op_annot::_here_block {idx} {
+  if {[catch {xschem get schname} s]} { return {} }
+  set k [::op_annot::_normkey $s]
+  if {$k ne {} && [dict exists $idx $k]} { return [dict get $idx $k] }
+  return {}
+}
+
+## Is instance <i> in the deck, i.e. may it have a save card?
+## <idx> is op_annot::_deck_index's answer, passed as a VALUE rather than read
+## from a hidden armed variable so a test can put the truth table on the page.
+proc op_annot::_netlisted {i idx} {
+  if {[catch {xschem getprop instance $i name} nm]} { return 0 }
+  set blk [::op_annot::_here_block $idx]
+  if {[llength $blk] == 0} { return 0 }
+  foreach e [::op_annot::_elements $nm] {
+    if {[lsearch -exact $blk $e] >= 0} { return 1 }
+  }
+  return 0
+}
+
+## May the walk DESCEND into instance <i>?  (decision D6, and it is NOT an alias
+## of _netlisted — see the two-questions block in the header.)
+proc op_annot::_descendable {i idx} {
+  if {![::op_annot::_netlisted $i $idx]} { return 0 }
+  if {[catch {xschem get_sch_from_sym $i} c]} { return 0 }
+  set k [::op_annot::_normkey $c]
+  if {$k eq {}} { return 0 }
+  if {![dict exists $idx $k]} { return 0 }
+  return [expr {[llength [dict get $idx $k]] > 0}]
+}
+
+## Refuse while a modal gesture is pending (I4).
+##
+## ⚠ THIS IS THE SHARPEST SEAM OF THE ORACLE INVERSION. `xschem netlist` calls
+## leave_placement_for("Netlist") and leave_merge_for("Netlist")
+## (scheduler.c:8823-8824) whose teardown IS a delete() (issue 0263), and
+## neither is suppressible from Tcl. Without this gate a read-only annotation
+## menu item would silently destroy the symbol the user is carrying on the
+## cursor, or the paste they have not dropped yet. Masks are
+## START_SYMPIN|PLACE_SYMBOL|PLACE_TEXT (25600) and STARTMERGE (256), i.e.
+## exactly leave_placement_for()'s and leave_merge_for()'s own tests
+## (callback.c:933). Numeric literals are this tree's established Tcl idiom for
+## ui_state (src/xschem.tcl:6558/11000/11394, create_instance.tcl:38).
+##
+## REJECTED: inheriting `xschem netlist`'s gesture semantics — zero code, and it
+## opens precisely the door tests/headless/test_placement_preview_doors.tcl
+## exists to keep shut.
+proc op_annot::_assert_idle {} {
+  if {[catch {xschem get ui_state} st]} { return {} }
+  if {![string is integer -strict $st]} { return {} }
+  if {$st & 25600} {
+    return -code error "op_annot::save_cards: a symbol/text PLACEMENT is\
+ pending. This walk runs a netlist to find out which devices are in the deck,\
+ and the netlister tears a pending placement down (issue 0263). Drop or cancel\
+ it (Esc) first."
+  }
+  if {$st & 256} {
+    return -code error "op_annot::save_cards: a PASTE/merge is pending. This\
+ walk runs a netlist to find out which devices are in the deck, and the\
+ netlister tears a pending paste down (issue 0263). Drop or cancel it (Esc)\
+ first."
+  }
+  return {}
+}
+
+## The cards for ONE instance, or {} when it contributes none.
+##
+## ⚠ THE 0425 CONSUMER CONTRACT (see the header): skip on a blank DEVPATH, never
+## on a blank DESCRIPTOR. A descriptor whose `match` globs do not claim this
+## cell is still fully registered; emitting its params against an empty device
+## name would write `.save [id]` cards.
+##
+## ⚠ `deck` BASIS, ALWAYS (issue 0436). The root is threaded from save_cards'
+## entry level; nothing here does path arithmetic of its own.
+##
+## ⚠ NO CATCH. devpath returns {} for every DATA condition, so anything that
+## raises in here is a caller bug or a broken PDK three levels down, and I6's
+## whole point is that such a raise reaches save_cards' restore.
+##
+## `derived` and `pinexpr` are not iterated: neither has a vector in any raw
+## (spec §4.3). `derived` is computed from params after they are read, `pinexpr`
+## is an expression over pin voltages.
+proc op_annot::_cards_for {instname root} {
+  set t [::op_annot::type $instname]
+  if {$t eq {}} { return {} }
+  set d [::op_annot::descriptor $t]
+  if {![dict exists $d params]} { return {} }
+  set dev [::op_annot::devpath $instname deck $root]
+  if {$dev eq {}} { return {} }
+  set out {}
+  foreach row [dict get $d params] {
+    lappend out ".save ${dev}\[[lindex $row 1]\]"
+  }
+  return $out
+}
+
+## Did the descend just issued actually land us in a USABLE child?
+## <c0> is `xschem get currsch` from immediately BEFORE the descend.
+##
+## THIS IS THE 0250 CLASS SPLIT (see the header). Return value of `xschem
+## descend` is deliberately not consulted: it is 0 for both classes.
+##   currsch unchanged  -> class 1, nothing was pushed. NO go_back — the
+##                         prototype's unconditional one pops a caller's level.
+##   currsch advanced, descend_error set -> class 2 (`load-failed`): the
+##                         hierarchy ALREADY advanced, so this one DOES owe a
+##                         go_back before the walk can continue.
+##   currsch advanced, descend_error empty -> success.
+proc op_annot::_descended {c0} {
+  if {[catch {xschem get currsch} c1]} { return 0 }
+  if {$c1 <= $c0} { return 0 }
+  if {[catch {xschem get descend_error} derr]} { set derr {} }
+  if {$derr eq {}} { return 1 }
+  catch {xschem go_back 2}
+  return 0
+}
+
+## The recursion. Ported from sg13g2_hier_sch_expand (sg13g2_procs.tcl:366-421).
+## Appends to the namespace accumulator and returns nothing useful; a raise
+## anywhere inside it is caught exactly once, by save_cards.
+##
+## <root> is the walk's ENTRY sch_path, threaded down unchanged: it is the basis
+## every card is rooted at. <idx> is the deck index, built ONCE by save_cards —
+## one netlist per call, never one per instance.
+proc op_annot::_walk {root idx} {
+  variable _acc
+  variable warnings
+  variable _miss_dev
+  variable _miss_sub
+  global nolist_libs
+  set ninstances [xschem get instances]
+  for {set i 0} {$i < $ninstances} {incr i} {
+    set instname [xschem getprop instance $i name]
+    ## ⚠ `getprop instance <n> cell::type`, NOT `getprop symbol <cell> type`.
+    ## The symbol form RAISES for generator cells (measured: 6 of the 14
+    ## instances of sky130_tests/test_generators) and is the literal trigger of
+    ## issue 0431. The index form resolves for a vector instance name too.
+    set type [::op_annot::type $i]
+
+    ## nolist_libs, verbatim from the prototype: libraries the user has excluded
+    ## from hierarchy traversal (empty in every stock workarea).
+    set skip 0
+    if {[info exists nolist_libs] && [llength $nolist_libs]} {
+      if {[catch {abs_sym_path [xschem getprop instance $i cell::name]} abs_symbol]} {
+        set abs_symbol {}
+      }
+      foreach j $nolist_libs {
+        if {[catch {regexp $j $abs_symbol} hit]} { set hit 0 }
+        if {$hit} { set skip 1 ; break }
+      }
+    }
+    if {$skip} { continue }
+
+    ## Emit BEFORE descending: depth-first, parents before children. Order is
+    ## part of this step's golden. A device the deck does not contain gets no
+    ## card and is COUNTED (N8) — silence there is how two attempts shipped.
+    if {[::op_annot::_netlisted $i $idx]} {
+      foreach c [::op_annot::_cards_for $instname $root] { lappend _acc $c }
+    } elseif {[::op_annot::devpath $instname deck $root] ne {}} {
+      incr _miss_dev
+    }
+
+    if {$type ne {subcircuit}} { continue }
+
+    ## D6: the deck may hold the CALL and not the body (spice_stop,
+    ## spice_sym_def, default_schematic=ignore, spice_primitive), and it may hold
+    ## a perfectly good body that THIS instance does not reach (a spice_ignore'd
+    ## caller of a shared cell). Both are "do not walk in".
+    if {![::op_annot::_descendable $i $idx]} { incr _miss_sub ; continue }
+
+    ## Vector instances: `expandlabel x2[1:0]` -> `x2[1],x2[0] 2` (never raises,
+    ## answers `x1 1` for a scalar). Descend ONCE, then walk the members with
+    ## change_sch_path, then go_back ONCE.
+    set ninst [lindex [split [xschem expandlabel $instname] { }] 1]
+    for {set n 1} {$n <= $ninst} {incr n} {
+      if {$n == 1} {
+        set c0 [xschem get currsch]
+        ## ⚠ `xschem select instance <i>` TOGGLES, and go_back leaves the
+        ## instance we came from selected — so unselect first or the next
+        ## descend refuses with `no-selection` / picks the wrong target.
+        ## `fast nodraw` is src/xschem.tcl:3717's cheaper form: no status
+        ## traffic, no selection draw.
+        catch {xschem unselect_all}
+        catch {xschem select instance $i fast nodraw}
+        xschem descend $n 2
+        if {[catch {xschem get descend_error} derr]} { set derr {} }
+        if {![::op_annot::_descended $c0]} {
+          lappend warnings "cannot descend into $instname ($derr)"
+          break
+        }
+      }
+      if {$n > 1} { xschem change_sch_path $n }
+      ::op_annot::_walk $root $idx
+      if {$n == $ninst} { xschem go_back 2 }
+    }
+  }
+  return 1
+}
+
+## Ascend back to the level the walk started from — and NO FURTHER.
+##
+## Bounded by the ENTRY currsch, not by 0 (see I6 in the header). The guards are
+## because this runs on an unattended error path: a go_back that refuses to move
+## must break the loop rather than spin it forever.
+proc op_annot::_unwind {entry} {
+  set target [lindex $entry 0]
+  set guard 0
+  while {1} {
+    if {[catch {xschem get currsch} c]} { break }
+    if {$c <= $target} { break }
+    if {[catch {xschem go_back 2}]} { break }
+    if {[catch {xschem get currsch} c2]} { break }
+    if {$c2 >= $c} { break }
+    if {[incr guard] > 64} { break }
+  }
+}
+
+## THE UNCONDITIONAL RESTORE (I6). Every line is individually catch-wrapped so
+## one failure cannot skip the rest — the prototypes' straight-line reset is
+## simply not reached when the walk raises, which is issue 0431.
+##
+## <rc> is the catch code of the walk. It is deliberately NOT branched on: the
+## whole point is that this block runs identically on both paths. The netlist
+## environment is NOT restored here — the oracle gives it back itself, before
+## the walk ever starts, so it is already the user's during the walk.
+proc op_annot::_restore {entry rc} {
+  variable _busy
+  catch {::op_annot::_unwind $entry}
+  ## no_undo has no getter (issue 0432), so 0 is the only restorable value.
+  catch {xschem set no_undo 0}
+  catch {xschem set no_draw [lindex $entry 2]}
+  catch {set ::keep_symbols [lindex $entry 3]}
+  catch {xschem log_action -suppress pop}
+  set _busy 0
+}
+
+## `.save all` first (I2 / rule R2, DOT-card — see the header), then the cards.
+## No prose header: write_save_file adds the human one, and S4's deck consumer
+## would only have to strip it.
+proc op_annot::_block {cards} {
+  if {[llength $cards] == 0} { return {} }
+  return "[join [linsert $cards 0 {.save all}] \n]\n"
+}
+
+## op_annot::save_cards -> the `.save` block for the whole hierarchy below the
+## CURRENT cell, as text; {} when no device produced a card.
+##
+## The block is rooted HERE: every card is `deck`-based with this level's
+## `sch_path` as its root, so the file is a save block for a deck of the cell
+## you are standing in, and no loaded raw can move a single name (issue 0436).
+## It names ONLY devices the netlister itself put in that deck (issue 0442).
+##
+## ⚠ IT RE-RAISES. A partial block silently returned is the raw-destroying card
+## wearing a different hat: the devices the walk never reached are missing, and
+## the ones it did reach are not necessarily complete. Callers (including S4's
+## render_deck) must call this inside a catch.
+##
+## I4: reads context, never writes it. No instance placed, no set_modify,
+## nothing written to the .sch — and it refuses outright rather than let the
+## oracle's netlist eat a pending gesture.
+proc op_annot::save_cards {} {
+  variable _acc
+  variable warnings
+  variable _busy
+  variable _miss_dev
+  variable _miss_sub
+
+  ## FIRST, and before any state is touched: a nested call would overwrite the
+  ## outer walk's accumulator and its idea of the user's netlist settings, then
+  ## "restore" the forced ones (issue 0438).
+  if {[info exists _busy] && $_busy} {
+    return -code error "op_annot::save_cards: already running — the walk is not\
+ re-entrant (issue 0438). A nested call would overwrite the outer walk's cards\
+ and restore the netlist settings it forced."
+  }
+  ## SECOND: refuse on a pending gesture, before the oracle's netlist can eat it.
+  ::op_annot::_assert_idle
+
+  set _busy 1
+  set _acc {}
+  set warnings {}
+  set _miss_dev 0
+  set _miss_sub 0
+  set kd 0
+  if {[info exists ::keep_symbols]} { set kd $::keep_symbols }
+  set entry [list [xschem get currsch] [xschem get sch_path] \
+                  [xschem get no_draw] $kd]
+  ## THE ROOT OF THIS BLOCK. Read once, before anything moves.
+  set root [lindex $entry 1]
+  ## Pushed OUTSIDE the catch and popped inside _restore, so the pair survives
+  ## a raise. descend, go_back and netlist all self-log (actions.c:4073, :4229,
+  ## scheduler.c:8887) and a walk over a real design would otherwise flood a log
+  ## whose contract is REPLAYABLE USER EDITS.
+  catch {xschem log_action -suppress push}
+  set rc [catch {
+    ## THE ORACLE, ONCE, FIRST, AND AT THE ENTRY LEVEL — the deck the user would
+    ## simulate from here is the one whose devices these cards must name.
+    ## Before no_undo/no_draw/keep_symbols: the netlister does its own
+    ## push_undo/pop_undo round trip.
+    set idx [::op_annot::_deck_index [::op_annot::_oracle_deck]]
+    set ::keep_symbols 1
+    ## The prototype's entry unselect_all, kept: descend acts on the selection,
+    ## so this is a real precondition. The selection is NOT saved and restored —
+    ## `xschem selected_set` answers instance NAMES only, so a "restore" would
+    ## silently drop every wire and text the user had selected, which is more
+    ## surprising than clearing it. (The oracle's own netlist has already
+    ## cleared it: global_spice_netlist calls unselect_all twice.)
+    xschem unselect_all
+    xschem set no_draw 1
+    xschem set no_undo 1
+    ::op_annot::_walk $root $idx
+  } res opts]
+  ::op_annot::_restore $entry $rc
+  if {$rc} { return -options $opts $res }
+  ## The two aggregates (N8), appended after the walk's own per-cell descend
+  ## failures and emitted only when non-zero: a clean walk says nothing.
+  if {$_miss_dev > 0} {
+    lappend warnings "$_miss_dev device(s) claimed by a descriptor are not in\
+ the netlist and got no save card"
+  }
+  if {$_miss_sub > 0} {
+    ## ⚠ WORDING, NOT BEHAVIOUR (decision D14). This line fires on perfectly
+    ## HEALTHY designs — mips_cpu/controller reports 2, because maindec and
+    ## aludec are behavioural cells whose whole body is schprop Verilog, i.e.
+    ## genuinely empty `.subckt` blocks with nothing to save. It read as
+    ## breakage, so it now names the ordinary causes. The substring `expand` is
+    ## kept deliberately: rows T22 and S14 match on it.
+    ##
+    ## REJECTED: suppressing the count when a block is empty because the cell has
+    ## no instances. The walk would have to know WHY it is empty — i.e. re-derive
+    ## spice_stop / spice_sym_def / behavioural — which is the hand-maintained
+    ## mirror of the netlister this whole step exists to delete.
+    lappend warnings "$_miss_sub subcircuit instance(s) got no cards because the\
+ netlist does not expand them here (spice_stop, spice_sym_def, an ignored or\
+ behavioural cell, or an empty block) - normal for such cells"
+  }
+  return [::op_annot::_block $_acc]
+}
+
+## op_annot::write_save_file -> writes the block to
+## $netlist_dir/<current cell>.save and returns the path; {} when there was
+## nothing to save (and then NO file is written — a .save file containing only
+## a header says nothing while reporting success).
+##
+## ⚠ THE FILENAME AGREES WITH THE BODY, and only because the cards are
+## entry-relative (D2): `<current cell>.save` describes a deck whose top IS the
+## current cell, which is also the cell `xschem netlist` would write from here.
+##
+## Modelled on IHP's `Create FET and BIP .save file` menu command
+## (sg13g2_procs.tcl:602-606), including its `file mkdir $netlist_dir`, which
+## sky130_procs.tcl:235 lacks. The textwindow is X-only: under --nogui / --pipe
+## there is no Tk and `textwindow` would raise.
+##
+## Definition only at source time — the SOURCE-TIME RULE above. Nothing here
+## runs until a menu item or a script calls it.
+proc op_annot::write_save_file {} {
+  global netlist_dir
+  set block [::op_annot::save_cards]
+  if {$block eq {}} { return {} }
+  file mkdir $netlist_dir
+  set path [file join $netlist_dir \
+    "[file rootname [file tail [xschem get current_name]]].save"]
+  ## THE WARNINGS REACH THE USER HERE, AND NOWHERE ELSE (decision D13).
+  ##
+  ## The two aggregates and the per-cell descend failures exist because a filter
+  ## that UNDER-emits IN SILENCE is exactly the failure that survived 85 and then
+  ## 96 green checks — and until now they were computed and thrown away: nothing
+  ## read op_annot::last_warnings. The textwindow this proc already opens is the
+  ## channel, at zero new UI.
+  ##
+  ## REJECTED: an `alert_` on success. X-only, untestable headless, and it would
+  ## fire on healthy designs (see the _miss_sub note in save_cards). REJECTED
+  ## also: leaving them unsurfaced, which reproduces at the UI layer the very
+  ## silence this step exists to delete.
+  ##
+  ## ⚠ ONE LINE PER WARNING, NEWLINES FLATTENED. A note that wrapped would put a
+  ## continuation line with no leading `*` into a file a SPICE parser reads, and
+  ## the parser would take it for a card.
+  set notes {}
+  foreach w [::op_annot::last_warnings] {
+    append notes "* NOTE: [string map [list \n { } \r { }] $w]\n"
+  }
+  ## ASCII only: these lines go into a file a SPICE parser reads.
+  write_data "* operating-point .save cards - .include this file in your testbench\n$notes\n$block" $path
+  if {[info exists ::has_x] && $::has_x} { catch {textwindow $path} }
+  return $path
+}
