+# =============================================================================
+# SECTION T — S3c: THE NETLISTER IS THE ORACLE, NOT A TCL MIRROR OF IT
+# =============================================================================
+# Section S above is attempt 2's suite, re-applied. It is green on a filter that
+# knows THREE of the SPICE netlister's drop classes. This section is the reason
+# attempt 3 exists: it asks the netlister itself which instances are in the deck,
+# on a HIERARCHICAL fixture that carries all EIGHT classes, and it is the row set
+# a hand-maintained mirror cannot pass.
+#
+# ============================================================================
+# WHY A MIRROR IS WRONG BY CONSTRUCTION (issue 0442, and the whole of this step)
+# ============================================================================
+# Attempt 2's `op_annot::_netlisted` hand-coded spice_ignore / only_toplevel /
+# lvs_ignore and missed four SYMBOL-level classes that live in
+# spice_block_netlist() — empty `format` (spice_netlist.c:639),
+# `default_schematic=ignore` (:643), `spice_sym_def` (:665), `spice_stop=true`
+# (:635+:695) — plus an EIGHTH, `spice_primitive=true` (:502), that NEITHER
+# attempt filtered. Under the dot-card idiom this feature generates, ONE card for
+# a device the deck does not contain makes ngspice write NO RAW AT ALL at rc=0,
+# on both installed binaries (issue 0442's re-measurement). So the filter is not
+# a nicety; a wrong filter destroys the data the feature exists to display.
+#
+# The inversion: run the netlist the design would actually simulate, READ which
+# instances appear in it, and emit cards only for those. Measured on this tree,
+# it collapses all eight classes into TWO questions with no per-class Tcl rule
+# anywhere:
+#
+#   _netlisted    is my element name in MY PARENT CELL'S block?      -> cards
+#   _descendable  that, AND does a NON-EMPTY block exist for my       -> descent
+#                 child's `** sch_path:`?
+#
+# ============================================================================
+# THE SEAMS THIS SECTION PINS, AND WHY EACH IS A SEPARATE NAME
+# ============================================================================
+#   op_annot::_oracle_dir {}       the directory the throwaway deck is written
+#                                  into. A NAME so a test can prove no temp file
+#                                  survives (T15) and can force the "no deck was
+#                                  written" path (T16) without a broken PDK.
+#   op_annot::_oracle_deck {}      runs the netlist, returns the deck TEXT, and
+#                                  RAISES when no deck was written. Never returns
+#                                  an empty string on failure — that would emit
+#                                  zero cards and read to the user as "this PDK
+#                                  is not supported" (decision N6).
+#   op_annot::_deck_index {text}   text -> dict: normalized absolute .sch path ->
+#                                  list of lowercased element names.
+#   op_annot::_normkey {path}      the ONE path normalizer, both sides of every
+#                                  comparison (T19 is its guardian).
+#   op_annot::_element {inst}      lowercased `@spiceprefix@name` — the
+#                                  instance's identity IN THE DECK.
+#   op_annot::_netlisted {i idx}   membership, against the CURRENT cell's block.
+#   op_annot::_descendable {i idx} membership AND a non-empty child block.
+#   op_annot::_assert_idle {}      refuses while a placement/paste is pending.
+#   op_annot::_restore_netlist_env restores the forced netlist globals.
+#   op_annot::_deck_user_region    the parser's `**** begin/end user architecture
+#                                  code` region tracker.
+#
+# ⚠ THE `idx` ARGUMENT IS DELIBERATE. `_netlisted` and `_descendable` take the
+# index as a VALUE rather than reading a hidden armed variable, so a row can put
+# the truth table on the page (T4/T5) instead of inferring it from a card list.
+# The index is still built ONCE per save_cards — one netlist per call, not one
+# per instance.
+#
+# ============================================================================
+# ⚠ THE FIXTURE IS THE POINT. ALL EIGHT CLASSES, HIERARCHICAL, MEASURED.
+# ============================================================================
+# ptop.sch (11 instances) + a shared 2-deep subtree. `xschem netlist` on it,
+# measured on this tree with this binary, writes:
+#
+#   **.subckt ptop
+#   XMTOP  XMLVS  XMOTL  xplain  xstop  xsymdef  xign  xprim      <- and NOTHING
+#   **.ends                                        for MIGN, xsigign, xnofmt
+#   .subckt psub  : XMA xleaf        (MOTL2 dropped: only_toplevel below the top)
+#   .subckt pstop : EMPTY            (spice_stop)
+#   .subckt pleaf : XMB
+#   psymdef       : NO BLOCK AT ALL — the body is the attribute text, and there
+#                   is no `** sch_path:` line, which is exactly why the index is
+#                   keyed on `** sch_path:`
+#   pign, pprim   : no block (default_schematic=ignore / spice_primitive)
+#
+# Every class lands on a DIFFERENT (inParent, childblock, non-empty) combination,
+# so the two gates cannot be aliases and each sabotage variant reds a different
+# row set. Spec landmine 11: a predicted red that does not appear is a FIXTURE
+# defect, and that is precisely how 96 green checks certified a broken filter.
+#
+# ⚠ THE PROCESS EXIT CODE. `xschem netlist` writes the global exit_code
+# (spice_netlist.c:611, consumed by xinit.c:3893) and there is no Tcl seam to
+# save or restore it — issue 0444. This file is immune only because it ends with
+# an explicit `exit`; a suite that falls off the end after an oracle run exits
+# with the netlister's code instead of its own.
+
+# --- section T fixture locations ---------------------------------------------
+set T_LIB2 [file join $scratch lib2]
+file mkdir $T_LIB2 [file join $scratch tnl]
+
+## The 8-class fixture, written fresh. `p*` names: pdev is the device, and one
+## subcircuit symbol per drop class.
+proc opa_t_symtxt {extra} {
+  return "v {xschem version=3.4.4 file_version=1.2}
+G {type=subcircuit
+format=\"@name @pinlist @symname\"
+template=\"name=x1\"
+$extra}
+V {}
+S {}
+E {}
+L 4 -20 -20 20 -20 {}
+B 5 -22.5 -2.5 -17.5 2.5 {name=A dir=inout}"
+}
+proc opa_t_sch {body} {
+  return "v {xschem version=3.4.4 file_version=1.2}
+G {}
+V {}
+S {}
+E {}
+$body"
+}
+proc opa_t_write {path txt} { set fd [open $path w] ; puts $fd $txt ; close $fd }
+
+## ⚠ spiceprefix lives in the SYMBOL TEMPLATE and nowhere on the instance lines —
+## the issue 0430 shape, and the reason T21 exists: `getprop instance <n>
+## spiceprefix` answers {} here while the deck element really is `XMTOP`.
+opa_t_write [file join $lib pdev.sym] {v {xschem version=3.4.4 file_version=1.2}
+G {type=nmos
+format="@spiceprefix@name @pinlist @model"
+template="name=M1 model=pmodel spiceprefix=X"}
+V {}
+S {}
+E {}
+L 4 -20 -20 20 -20 {}
+B 5 -22.5 -2.5 -17.5 2.5 {name=D dir=inout}}
+opa_t_write [file join $lib psub.sym]    [opa_t_symtxt {}]
+opa_t_write [file join $lib pleaf.sym]   [opa_t_symtxt {}]
+opa_t_write [file join $lib pstop.sym]   [opa_t_symtxt {spice_stop=true}]
+opa_t_write [file join $lib psymdef.sym] [opa_t_symtxt "spice_sym_def=\"Rfake A 0 1k\""]
+opa_t_write [file join $lib pign.sym]    [opa_t_symtxt {default_schematic=ignore}]
+opa_t_write [file join $lib pprim.sym]   [opa_t_symtxt {spice_primitive=true}]
+## class 1: an EMPTY `format` — spice_netlist.c:639 returns before anything is
+## written, so the instance is absent from the deck ENTIRELY.
+opa_t_write [file join $lib pnofmt.sym] {v {xschem version=3.4.4 file_version=1.2}
+G {type=subcircuit
+format=""
+template="name=x1"}
+V {}
+S {}
+E {}
+L 4 -20 -20 20 -20 {}
+B 5 -22.5 -2.5 -17.5 2.5 {name=A dir=inout}}
+
+opa_t_write [file join $lib ptop.sch] [opa_t_sch {C {pdev.sym} 0 0 0 0 {name=MTOP}
+C {pdev.sym} 100 0 0 0 {name=MIGN spice_ignore=true}
+C {pdev.sym} 200 0 0 0 {name=MLVS lvs_ignore=true}
+C {pdev.sym} 300 0 0 0 {name=MOTL only_toplevel=true}
+C {psub.sym} 0 100 0 0 {name=xplain}
+C {psub.sym} 100 100 0 0 {name=xsigign spice_ignore=true}
+C {pstop.sym} 200 100 0 0 {name=xstop}
+C {psymdef.sym} 300 100 0 0 {name=xsymdef}
+C {pign.sym} 400 100 0 0 {name=xign}
+C {pnofmt.sym} 500 100 0 0 {name=xnofmt}
+C {pprim.sym} 600 100 0 0 {name=xprim}}]
+## psub is SHARED by xplain and by the spice_ignore'd xsigign — that sharing is
+## what makes the two gates genuinely diverge (T5), and a flat fixture cannot
+## express it.
+opa_t_write [file join $lib psub.sch] [opa_t_sch {C {pdev.sym} 0 0 0 0 {name=MA}
+C {pdev.sym} 100 0 0 0 {name=MOTL2 only_toplevel=true}
+C {pleaf.sym} 200 0 0 0 {name=xleaf}}]
+## Each dropped class's schematic holds one device the descriptor claims, so a
+## walk that wrongly descends into it produces a VISIBLE extra card rather than
+## a silent no-op.
+foreach t {pstop psymdef pign pnofmt pprim} {
+  opa_t_write [file join $lib $t.sch] [opa_t_sch {C {pdev.sym} 0 0 0 0 {name=MA}}]
+}
+opa_t_write [file join $lib pleaf.sch] [opa_t_sch {C {pdev.sym} 0 0 0 0 {name=MB}}]
+
+## The same-basename collision (issue 0445): lib2/psub.sym is a DIFFERENT cell
+## with the same basename. global_spice_netlist hashes blocks by get_cell()
+## (spice_netlist.c:487), so the deck gets ONE `.subckt psub` and lib2's device
+## is nowhere in it.
+opa_t_write [file join $T_LIB2 psub.sym] [opa_t_symtxt {}]
+opa_t_write [file join $T_LIB2 psub.sch] [opa_t_sch {C {pdev.sym} 0 0 0 0 {name=MDUP}}]
+opa_t_write [file join $lib pcoll.sch] [opa_t_sch {C {psub.sym} 0 0 0 0 {name=xa}
+C {../lib2/psub.sym} 200 0 0 0 {name=xb}}]
+## The polymorphic cell: xp2 overrides psub.sym's schematic with pleaf.sch.
+opa_t_write [file join $lib ppoly.sch] [opa_t_sch {C {psub.sym} 0 0 0 0 {name=xp1}
+C {psub.sym} 200 0 0 0 {name=xp2 schematic=pleaf.sch}}]
+## The SAME library reached two ways: `psub.sym` and `../lib/psub.sym`.
+opa_t_write [file join $lib pdot2.sch] [opa_t_sch {C {psub.sym} 0 0 0 0 {name=xa}
+C {../lib/psub.sym} 200 0 0 0 {name=xb}}]
+## A netlist_commands/code symbol whose text FABRICATES an element line for the
+## spice_ignore'd GHOST. Measured live on this tree — see T23.
+opa_t_write [file join $lib puser.sch] "v {xschem version=3.4.4 file_version=1.2}
+G {}
+V {}
+S {}
+E {}
+C {pdev.sym} 0 0 0 0 {name=MREAL}
+C {pdev.sym} 100 0 0 0 {name=GHOST spice_ignore=true}
+C {devices/code_shown.sym} 300 0 0 0 {name=CODE only_toplevel=false value=\"xghost net1 pmodel
+.param zz=1\"}"
+
+## The T32b fixture: a user-architecture region that CONTAINS A COMPLETE
+## `.subckt`/`.ends` PAIR, with a real device placed AFTER it in the same cell.
+##
+## ⚠ IT HAS TO BE A SUBCIRCUIT, AND `schprop` WILL NOT DO — both measured on
+## this tree while writing this row, and both correct the plan's wording.
+##   * schprop is emitted by spice_block_netlist AFTER every element line and
+##     immediately before `.ends` (spice_netlist.c:698-704), so nothing of the
+##     cell's own follows it and the early close has nothing left to eat;
